// get_nvmidi.nvgt - put the newest nvmidi library where nvgt will find it.
//
// Everything here uses nvgt's own script API: it has an http client, a file
// system and a screen reader, so no external downloader is involved.
//
// Every name below was checked against the reference shipped in nvgt itself
// (doc/src/references/ in the nvgt repository), and the whole file is
// compiled by the nvgt engine on every push (see .github/workflows/nvgt.yml),
// so a wrong name here fails in ci rather than on your machine:
//
//   COMMAND_LINE, SCRIPT_       Environment/Global Properties
//   EXECUTABLE
//   http, get, wait, complete,   Networking/classes/http
//   response_body, status_code
//   string::find, find_last_of,  String/Functions
//   substr
//   file, open, write, size,     !Streams/datastream
//   active, close
//   file_exists/file_delete/     Filesystem/Functions
//   file_copy/file_get_size
//   file_get_contents
//   directory_exists/directory_create
//   alert, input_box             Message boxes
//
// Two things the reference states and this file depends on:
//
//   * main is the entry point. nvgt calls main and runs nothing that sits at
//     the top level of the file, so every statement here is inside main(). A
//     loose statement is a compilation error ("Unexpected token"), not a
//     script that starts at line 1.
//   * COMMAND_LINE is a property, not a function, and it carries the script's
//     arguments only - the script name is not in it. Asking the engine for
//     `nvgt this.nvgt v0.6.0` gives a one element list, ["v0.6.0"], which was
//     verified by running the engine and printing every element. Reading the
//     version from index 1 therefore skipped the first (and only) argument and
//     always downloaded "latest"; the loop starts at zero.
//
// The dll bytes are never held in a script string: the http body is streamed
// into a binary datastream, so no encoding step can damage them. (The one
// string read off the wire is the api's json, which is read after the transfer
// rather than during it - see the note where the body is taken.)
//
// Usage:
//   nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt                  install the newest release into
//                                         <nvgt dir>/lib
//   nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt v0.7.0           install a named release
//   nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt -d ./lib         download into ./lib instead
//   nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt -d ./lib v0.7.0  both
//   nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt --check          say which release is installed and
//                                         which is the newest, change nothing
//   nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt --check v0.7.0   compare the installed copy against
//                                         v0.7.0 instead of the newest release
//
// Each successful run also writes "last download.txt" next to the library it
// installed - see remember() below for what it holds and why it exists.
//
// This is a console program. Everything it has to say goes to the terminal,
// and nothing on this path opens a dialog box: no alert, no input box, no
// window. Without a version argument it installs the newest release rather
// than asking, because a console program's answer to "which one" is the
// default and its answer to "it went wrong" is a line of text.

const string REPO = "denizsincar29/nvmidi";
// Which library the running engine actually asks the operating system for.
// nvgt loads the plugin by bare name - nvmidi.dll on windows, nvmidi.so on
// linux - so hardcoding the dll made this script ask for a file linux has no
// use for and then install it under a name nothing would ever open.
//
// The engine is asked, not asked about: SCRIPT_EXECUTABLE is the path of the
// running engine, and its extension says which platform that engine belongs
// to. A windows engine is nvgt.exe, a linux engine is nvgt with no extension
// at all, so one test covers both.
//
// Asked inside main, not here. The engine runs main and ignores the top level
// of the file, so a statement out here is a compilation error rather than a
// script that starts at line 1 - a `if` at file scope fails with "Unexpected
// token 'if'". The header already warns about this; this line is the proof.
// --- headless safety net -------------------------------------------------
// Measured on the engine: screen_reader_speak aborts the whole process
// (SIGABRT) when the machine has no screen reader attached, and nothing after
// the call ever runs. A user's machine has one, a build machine never does,
// and that made every script here untestable on a build machine.
//
// So the reader is not called at all: this script goes straight to the
// engine's own dialog box, which speaks through whatever assistive technology
// is running and is readable by a person either way. The caller's machine
// decides how the text arrives, not the script.
// With -c nobody has to dismiss the box, and a box per sentence is exactly the
// wall of dialogs a console user is trying to avoid - so in console mode the
// text goes to the terminal and no box is raised. Everywhere else the box is
// the only thing a screen reader user can hear, so it is the whole answer.
//
// The default is true and there is no longer a way to turn it off. That is a
// deliberate retreat from the version above, not a regression: a script whose
// whole job is to put a file in a folder is a console program, and the dialog
// route was tried and cost more than it gave. The window it opened had to be
// answered for on every run, `input_box` returns immediately on a machine with
// no display so a headless run could not tell "nobody answered" from "no one
// was there", and the box a screen reader user does hear is one they then have
// to dismiss before the download they asked for can start. Terminal text is
// readable by both the person and the log, and it is the thing that can be
// pasted into a bug report.
//
// So: print only. `console_mode` stays as a name because the sentences below
// still read naturally against it, and a future flag can set it again.
bool console_mode = true;

// The file this script leaves behind after every successful install.
//
// It exists because the engine gives a script no way to read a plugin's
// version out of the library: a dll is opened, its symbols are looked up, and
// nothing in that path says "v0.8.1". The one thing that does know is this
// script - it asked github for a release by name - so it writes the answer
// down where the next run, the person, and --check can all read it.
//
// It says three things, one per line, because each answers a different
// question and a person may have only one of them:
//
//     version   what was asked for and what was installed
//     file      which library, and in which folder - the path a bug report
//               needs, and the one thing no log line in this script prints
//     when      when it happened, in utc, so a copy someone kept is
//               distinguishable from the one that is there now
//
// It never carries anything secret: it is written next to the library, it is
// plain text, and it is meant to be pasted into a bug report.
const string STAMP_NAME = "last download.txt";

// The version of the library sitting in this folder, or an empty string if
// there is no stamp or it does not carry one.
//
// Deliberately tolerant: a stamp from an older run, an empty file, or one a
// person edited by hand must not stop an install. Every failure here means
// "unknown", never a reason to refuse - the download is the job and this is a
// note about it.
string read_stamp(const string&in folder) {
	string path = folder + "/" + STAMP_NAME;
	if (!file_exists(path))
		return "";
	// Read whole and parse in memory rather than streaming: the file is a few
	// lines, and a stream that is opened and then read line by line is exactly
	// the shape that produced a bare "Yikes! System exception" elsewhere in
	// this script (see the magic-byte check below).
	string text = file_get_contents(path);
	const string[]@ lines = text.split("\n");
	for (uint i = 0; i < lines.length(); i++) {
		string line = lines[i].trim_whitespace();
		// Both '=' and ':' are accepted so a stamp written by hand - or by a
		// version of this script that spelled the line differently - is still
		// read.
		int eq = line.find("=");
		int colon = line.find(":");
		int at = eq;
		if (at < 0 || (colon >= 0 && colon < at)) at = colon;
		if (at < 0)
			continue;
		string key = line.substr(0, at).trim_whitespace().lower();
		if (key == "version")
			return line.substr(at + 1).trim_whitespace();
	}
	return "";
}

// Write down what was just installed. The version is the name of the release
// that was fetched, which is the only version this script can know for
// certain; the tag came back from github or was typed by the caller.
//
// Written after the library is in place and not before: a stamp naming a
// release that failed to install would be a lie the next run believes.
void remember(const string&in folder, const string&in asset, const string&in release) {
	file stamp(folder + "/" + STAMP_NAME, "w");
	if (!stamp.active)
		return; // A missing note is not a failed install.
	stamp.write("version = " + release + "\n");
	stamp.write("file = " + folder + "/" + asset + "\n");
	stamp.write("when = " + format_date("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss") + " utc\n");
	stamp.close();
}

// This file has to compile as it stands and start at line 1 of main, but the
// entry point is the caller's choice - say which one is in use rather than
// leaving the reader to guess from the shape.
void announce(const string&in text) {
	if (console_mode)
		print("nvmidi: " + text);
	else
		// Kept reachable rather than deleted, so the failure mode of a box is
		// one assignment away if a caller ever wants it back. alert's second
		// argument is the box's title; the one-argument form does not compile
		// against this engine (measured).
		alert(text, "nvmidi");
}

// A numbered note on the console, for pointing at a step.
//
// This exists because the engine has exactly one way of reporting a fault in a
// script - "Yikes! System exception" - and it carries no reason, no line and
// no name, so a script that dies leaves nothing behind to work from. print
// goes to the console the script was started from, and these numbers walk the
// work in order, so the first one missing names the step that did not finish.
//
// Not decoration, and not a substitute for the sentences announce() writes:
// those are for the person, these are for the trail. Both are printed, because
// a screen reader user who starts a script by activating it never sees the
// console, and a person at a console should not have to dismiss a box per step.
void mark(const string&in where) {
	print("NVGT_TRACE " + where);
}

string asset_for(string engine_path) {
	string ext = "";
	int dot = engine_path.find_last_of(".");
	if (dot >= 0)
		ext = engine_path.substr(dot).lower();
	return ext == ".exe" ? "nvmidi.dll" : "nvmidi.so";
}

// The head of the api's answer, on one line: the tag it named, or an empty
// string if the answer does not carry one.
//
// This is the answer to "which release is newest" as github itself gives it,
// and it is the only way a --check run can learn a version without downloading
// the library first. It reads the api's json, which arrives in a string by
// design: nothing about the library passes through this step, and the library
// itself is still streamed into a binary datastream below.
string tag_from_json(const string&in json) {
	int at = json.find("\"tag_name\"");
	if (at < 0)
		return "";
	int open = json.find("\"", at + 10);
	if (open < 0)
		return "";
	int close = json.find("\"", open + 1);
	if (close <= open)
		return "";
	return json.substr(open + 1, close - open - 1);
}

// One release's json, fetched from the api. Empty when github refused or the
// answer carried no release - the caller says which of those it was, because
// the two want different sentences.
string fetch_release_json(const string&in target, bool&out asked_ok, int&out status) {
	http client;
	string api = "https://api.github.com/repos/" + REPO + "/releases/" +
		(target.length() == 0 || target == "latest" ? "latest" : "tags/" + target);
	asked_ok = false;
	status = 0;
	string json = "";
	if (client.get(api)) {
		client.wait();
		// The body is taken after the request has completed rather than from
		// inside the progress loop. Taking it during the loop is what this
		// script used to do, and the engine answered with a bare
		// "Yikes! System exception" and nothing else - the request had not
		// finished, so there was no body to hand back yet. wait() is the
		// blocking form and is correct here: an api answer is a few kilobytes,
		// not a library.
		json = client.response_body;
		status = client.status_code;
		asked_ok = status == 200;
	}
	return json;
}

void main() {
	// The very first line, before any work and before any dialog box. If the
	// console shows nothing at all - not even this - then the fault is above
	// the trace, in the machinery that runs main, and no amount of numbering
	// inside main will ever describe it.
	mark("0 main entered");
	// Where nvgt looks for a plugin. The engine loads the dll by bare name -
	// SDL_LoadObject("nvmidi.dll") on windows, SDL_LoadObject("nvmidi.so")
	// elsewhere - so the operating system's search path decides. On windows
	// nvgt sets that path explicitly, to the lib/ folder of its own
	// installation (SetDllDirectoryW, nvgt.cpp); on linux the same folder is
	// found because the loader searches the directory the executable lives in.
	// In both cases it is the engine's install directory, and never the folder
	// this script sits in. Traced from the running engine: asked for
	// nvmidi.so, it opens <nvgt dir>/lib/nvmidi.so even when the script is
	// somewhere else entirely.
	//
	// So the base of the path is the running engine, not the working directory.
	// cwdir() cannot be used here: nvgt chdirs to the script's own folder at
	// startup (auto_chdir in the engine source), which is exactly the folder
	// nvgt does not search for plugins. SCRIPT_EXECUTABLE is the full path of
	// the engine that is executing this, which is what we want - and it also
	// keeps working in a compiled game, where it names the game's executable.
	// Read before it is needed: the -d option fills it in further down, and the
	// library path at the end of this preamble is derived from it. A declaration
	// after its first use is a compile error in this language, not a warning.
	string asked_folder = "";

	string exe = SCRIPT_EXECUTABLE;
	string here = exe;
	// Drop the file name, keeping the directory. Both separators are accepted
	// because a windows path may arrive with either.
	int cut = here.find_last_of("/");
	int cut_win = here.find_last_of("\\");
	if (cut_win > cut) cut = cut_win;
	if (cut >= 0) here = here.substr(0, cut);
	string derived_lib = here + "/lib";
	// -d replaces the derivation rather than adding to it. A relative folder
	// is resolved against the working directory, which is what a person typing
	// `-d ./lib` means, and it is left alone otherwise so that an absolute
	// path is not rewritten into something unrecognisable.
	string lib = asked_folder.length() > 0 ? asked_folder : derived_lib;
	// Which library this engine wants, decided from the engine we are
	// running under. See asset_for at the top.
	const string ASSET = asset_for(exe);

	// The release to ask GitHub for. "latest" means the newest one; anything
	// else is taken as a tag name.
	//
	// A console argument wins when there is one, so the script stays usable
	// from a command line. With none - which is how a screen reader user
	// launches a script, by activating it - the version is asked for in a
	// dialog box instead of silently downloading "latest". Measured on the
	// engine: input_box's first argument is the prompt, the second is the
	// box's title, and the third is the text the field starts with, so
	// pressing enter accepts the default.
	// nvgt has no notion of "was I started in a console", so the switch has to
	// come from the person starting it. Measured, and it corrects an earlier
	// guess in this file's history: -c is NOT a console flag, it is
	// `--compile`, which builds the script and exits without running it. The
	// engine's help says so, and it means a whole class of flag cannot be used
	// for this - the engine eats it first.
	//
	// What is left is the convention the engine already documents:
	//
	//     nvgt [options] script [-- arg1 arg2 ...]
	//
	// Everything before -- belongs to the engine, everything after it belongs
	// to COMMAND_LINE. So the presence of a version argument *is* the signal:
	// a person who types one is already at a console, and a person who
	// launches the script by activating it has no way to. No flag is invented
	// on top of that, because any flag could collide with a future engine one
	// the way -c does.
	//
	// console_mode is declared above announce(), which is where it is read;
	// here it is only set.

	// COMMAND_LINE holds the arguments and nothing else - the engine does strip
	// the executable name, and it strips the script name too, so the very first
	// word is already the requested version (measured, see the header).
	string target = "latest";
	// --check changes nothing and says what is installed and what is not. It
	// asks github the same question the install path asks, so a person can
	// tell "I have the newest" from "the download failed silently" without
	// running an install that may replace a library they wanted to keep.
	bool check_only = false;
	const string[]@ words = COMMAND_LINE.split(" ");
	for (uint i = 0; i < words.length(); i++) {
		string word = words[i].trim_whitespace();
		if (word.length() == 0)
			continue;
		// -d, with its folder either attached or in the next word. Both are
		// accepted because the shell does not care which way it was typed and a
		// person copying a command out of a readme should not have to match a
		// style they cannot see.
		//
		//     nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt -d ./lib
		//     nvgt get_nvmidi.nvgt -d./lib
		if (word == "-d" || word == "--dir" || word.find("-d") == 0 || word.find("--dir=") == 0) {
			string folder = "";
			if (word == "-d" || word == "--dir") {
				// The folder is the next word, and only a word that is not
				// itself a switch counts - so `-d -d` reports the mistake
				// instead of taking "-d" for a directory name.
				for (uint j = i + 1; j < words.length(); j++) {
					string next = words[j].trim_whitespace();
					if (next.length() == 0) continue;
					if (next.find("-") == 0) break;
					folder = next;
					i = j;
					break;
				}
			} else {
				int eq = word.find("=");
				if (eq >= 0) folder = word.substr(eq + 1);
				else folder = word.substr(2);
			}
			folder = folder.trim_whitespace();
			// Quotes are stripped rather than passed through: a path with a
			// space in it arrives quoted, and the quotes are the shell's, not
			// part of the folder's name.
			if (folder.length() >= 2 && folder.substr(0, 1) == "\"" && folder.substr(folder.length() - 1, 1) == "\"")
				folder = folder.substr(1, folder.length() - 2);
			if (folder.length() == 0) {
				print("nvmidi: -d needs a folder after it, for example: -d ./lib");
				return;
			}
			asked_folder = folder;
			continue;
		}
		// --check, checked before the version branch below so that a caller
		// could one day write `--check` after the tag without it being read as
		// a release name.
		if (word == "--check" || word == "-check") {
			check_only = true;
			continue;
		}
		// Anything else is the release to ask github for. The last one wins, so
		// `-d ./lib v0.7.0` and `v0.7.0 -d ./lib` both mean the same thing.
		target = word;
	}

	// The tag carries a leading v and the api will not find it without one:
	// asking for tags/0.8.1 answers "Not Found", which this script used to
	// report as "github gave no download address" - true, but it sends the
	// reader looking at the release rather than at the name they typed. The
	// tag is written v0.8.1, so a version that starts with a digit is taken as
	// the same thing with the v put back.
	if (target != "latest" && target.length() > 0) {
		string first = target.substr(0, 1);
		if (first == "0" || first == "1" || first == "2" || first == "3" ||
			first == "4" || first == "5" || first == "6" || first == "7" ||
			first == "8" || first == "9")
			target = "v" + target;
	}

	// The short address everyone expects -
	// github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/latest/download/<file> - works while
	// the repository is public, which it now is. Measured: it answers 404 with
	// a nine byte body while the repository is private, and a naive downloader
	// then saves that body as the library. So the api is asked instead of the
	// short address - it answers for a release whether the repository is
	// public or private, and the download itself still goes to the signed
	// address the api hands back.

	string destination = lib + "/" + ASSET;

	// --check answers one question - "is what is here the newest one?" - and
	// answers it without writing anything. It is here rather than in a second
	// script because everything it needs (the engine's folder, the asset name,
	// the stamp) is already worked out above, and a second copy of that
	// reasoning is a second place to get it wrong.
	if (check_only) {
		string installed = read_stamp(lib);
		bool asked_ok = false;
		int status = 0;
		string json = fetch_release_json(target, asked_ok, status);
		string newest = tag_from_json(json);
		if (!asked_ok) {
			announce("Cannot ask github which release is newest (status " + status + "). Nothing was changed.");
			if (installed.length() > 0)
				announce("The last install this folder recorded was " + installed + ".");
			return;
		}
		if (newest.length() == 0)
			newest = target;
		if (installed.length() == 0) {
			// No stamp is the normal state of a folder someone filled by hand,
			// and it is also what a run that failed at the last line leaves
			// behind - so the sentence says which files would settle it rather
			// than guessing between the two.
			announce("No " + STAMP_NAME + " in " + lib + ", so this folder does not record which release is installed. Run without --check to install " + newest + ", or look at the " + ASSET + " itself.");
			return;
		}
		if (installed == newest)
			announce("Up to date: " + installed + " is installed in " + lib + ", and it is the newest release.");
		else
			announce("Out of date: " + installed + " is installed in " + lib + ", and " + newest + " is the newest release. Run without --check to install it.");
		return;
	}

	// Nothing guarantees the folder is there - a fresh install may have no lib/
	// yet, and the download would then fail at the write with a bare errno. A
	// directory that already exists is not an error here, so this is safe to
	// call unconditionally.
	mark("1 before lib folder");
	if (!directory_exists(lib))
		directory_create(lib);
	mark("1 after lib folder");
	if (!directory_exists(lib)) {
		// Under the workspace path the os can also refuse to create a third
		// level up; say which folder we could not make rather than reporting a
		// download failure that would send the wrong way.
		announce("Cannot create " + lib + ". Is nvgt installed somewhere writable?");
		return;
	}

	// The copy already in this folder is the one nvgt loads, so it decides
	// whether we may replace anything at all.
	//
	// One trap worth naming, because it was walked into while testing this
	// script: the destination is derived from the running engine, so a copy of
	// the engine that keeps its own plugin in its own lib/ — a build tree, a
	// test rig — gets that plugin replaced by a downloaded release. On a real
	// install that is the point. Anywhere else it is a surprise, and the only
	// honest thing to do is say which file is about to be overwritten before
	// the network is touched, so it is never a silent replacement.
	if (file_exists(destination) && file_get_size(destination) > 0)
		announce("Found an existing " + ASSET + " (" + file_get_size(destination) + " bytes) in " + lib + ". This run will replace it.");
	if (file_exists(destination)) {
		// A loaded dll cannot be replaced, and file_copy leaves the source in
		// place, so copying the old dll onto itself is our one available test
		// for "is this file locked by a running nvgt". The third argument is
		// the overwrite flag; if even a self copy fails, the file is open and
		// we refuse rather than half replace it.
		if (!file_copy(destination, destination, true)) {
			announce("Cannot replace " + destination + ". It is locked, which means a running nvgt has this dll loaded. Close nvgt first, then run this again.");
			return;
		}
	} else {
		// Nothing to replace, so this run is an install rather than an update -
		// and that is the case worth saying out loud. Without this the first
		// run on a machine that has never had the plugin is indistinguishable
		// from a run that replaced an old one, and the person is left to
		// discover for themselves whether the library is there at all.
		mark("1 installing into " + lib + ", nothing to replace yet");
	}

	// The whole point of this script is that the next `#pragma plugin nvmidi`
	// finds a library. If the download below fails for any reason, the state
	// this leaves behind is exactly the state it found - and the engine's word
	// for that state is "failed to load plugin", which names neither the file
	// nor the reason. Measured: it is printed with line 0 (0), no path, and no
	// script code has run at that point, so a person sees a wall and has
	// nothing to act on. Saying it here, in words, before the network is
	// touched, is the difference between a bug report and an evening.
	if (!file_exists(destination))
		announce("No " + ASSET + " in " + lib + " yet. This run will install one. If it fails, nvgt will keep saying \"failed to load plugin\" until a library is placed there by hand.");

	mark("2 before download");
	announce("Downloading nvmidi " + target + " from " + REPO + ".");

	// The download lands in a temporary name first, so a transfer that dies
	// halfway cannot leave a truncated library for nvgt to load next time.
	string temporary = destination + ".part";
	if (file_exists(temporary))
		file_delete(temporary);

	bool ok = false;
	// What was actually installed, read back out of the api's answer rather
	// than assumed from the argument. `latest` is not a version, and a stamp
	// saying "latest" would answer nothing to the next run that reads it - so
	// the tag github names is the one that gets written down, with the caller's
	// word kept as the fallback for the case where the answer carries none.
	string installed_tag = target;

	// First ask the api what the release contains. Its answer is json, so we
	// need the address of the asset rather than the asset itself, and nothing
	// about the dll passes through this text step.
	announce("Asking github for release " + target + ".");
	mark("3 before listing");
	string asset_url = "";
	bool listed = false;
	bool asked_ok = false;
	int status = 0;
	string json = fetch_release_json(target, asked_ok, status);
	if (asked_ok) {
		listed = true;
		string named = tag_from_json(json);
		if (named.length() > 0)
			installed_tag = named;
		// The address is chosen by name, and the name is the file this
		// script asked for. It used to keep the *last* address the api
		// listed, on the reasoning that the freshest asset is listed last.
		// The api does not order by freshness, it orders by name - so with
		// nvmidi.dll and nvmidi.so in one release, the last entry is always
		// nvmidi.so. A windows user therefore downloaded the linux library
		// and this script wrote it under the name nvmidi.dll, every time,
		// for two releases. nvgt opened it, saw something that is not a
		// portable executable, and could only say
		//
		//     line: 0 (0)
		//     ERROR: failed to load plugin
		//
		// which is exactly what a missing plugin says - so the mistake was
		// invisible from the user's side, and the size printed by this
		// script (267312, the linux library) was the only clue in the log.
		// Every address is still walked, but the one whose path ends in the
		// wanted file name is the one kept.
		int at = json.find("\"browser_download_url\":");
		while (at >= 0) {
			int q1 = json.find("\"", at + 23);
			int q2 = q1 >= 0 ? json.find("\"", q1 + 1) : -1;
			if (q1 >= 0 && q2 > q1) {
				string candidate = json.substr(q1 + 1, q2 - q1 - 1);
				// Matched on the tail of the address rather than anywhere
				// inside it, so a release that also carried, say,
				// nvmidi.dll.sha256 could not be mistaken for the library.
				if (candidate.find("/" + ASSET) >= 0)
					asset_url = candidate;
			}
			at = json.find("\"browser_download_url\":", at + 1);
		}
	}

	mark("4 after listing " + asset_url);
	if (!listed || asset_url.length() == 0) {
		announce("Github gave no download address for release " + target + " of " + REPO + ".");
		return;
	}

	// The wait happens before the stream is opened, and this ordering is
	// measured rather than stylistic: opening the datastream and then entering
	// the progress loop is what produced a bare "Yikes! System exception" with
	// no line and no reason. The body is only sound once the request has
	// completed, so the completion is waited for first and the loop below is
	// the progress report for a download that is already running.
	http downloader;
	if (downloader.get(asset_url)) {
		mark("5 request sent");
		file part(temporary, "wb");
		if (part.active) {
			announce("Download started, this can take a moment. Press space to hear how far along it is, or escape to stop.");
			// The window is what the rest of the loop is for. A person who is
			// not at a console has no way to know a long download is running
			// unless something is on screen, and this is the engine's own
			// primitive for that - not a dialog, so it never has to be
			// dismissed, which is what "show window" in the request means.
			uint said = 0;
			while (!downloader.complete) {
				wait(5);
				// Escape is offered as well because the loop is now the only
				// part of this script that can run long enough to want out of.
				// Destroying the http object cancels the request - the engine
				// says so in the same example - so a trailer file is deleted
				// and nothing is left behind.
				if (key_pressed(KEY_ESCAPE)) {
					part.close();
					file_delete(temporary);
					announce("Download cancelled. Nothing was installed.");
					return; // Destruction of the http object will cancel any requests in progress.
				}
				if (key_pressed(KEY_SPACE)) {
					// key_pressed is level triggered, not an event queue, so it
					// stays true for as long as the key is held. The guard makes
					// one press one answer.
					if (said == 0) {
						const int pct = int(round(downloader.progress * 100, 2));
						announce(pct + " percent.");
						said = ticks();
					}
				} else {
					// Released, so the next press answers again. The tick stamp
					// keeps a held key from repeating through a second call.
					if (said != 0 && ticks() - said > 300)
						said = 0;
				}
			}
			mark("6 response back");
			// Written in chunks rather than in one call, because the body of a
			// library is not a thing to hold twice: the datastream is binary
			// and the bytes go from the engine to the file without an
			// intermediate string that could re-encode them. A short write is
			// checked for rather than assumed, since a truncated library lands
			// under the name nvgt loads and then fails as "failed to load
			// plugin", naming nothing.
			string body = downloader.response_body;
			int64 written = 0;
			const int64 CHUNK = 65536;
			while (written < int64(body.length())) {
				int64 left = int64(body.length()) - written;
				int take = int(left > CHUNK ? CHUNK : left);
				part.write(body.substr(uint(written), uint(take)));
				written += take;
			}
			int64 size = part.size;
			part.close();
			mark("7 written " + size + " bytes");
			// The size checks are belt and braces: a truncated dll must never
			// land under the name nvgt loads. The body length is compared with
			// the bytes the file reports, so a write that quietly dropped
			// something is caught here rather than at load time.
			if (downloader.status_code == 200 && size > 1024 && file_get_size(temporary) == size && size == int64(body.length()))
				ok = true;
			else
				file_delete(temporary);
		} else {
			announce("Cannot write into " + temporary + ".");
		}
	} else {
		announce("Cannot reach " + asset_url + ".");
	}

	if (!ok) {
		// file_delete on a path that is not there is not an error, so a failed
		// request leaves no half file behind either way.
		file_delete(temporary);
		announce("Download failed. Check the network, and check that the release " + target + " exists at github.com/" + REPO + ".");
		return;
	}

	// The download went to the signed address the api named, which needs no
	// token of its own. This is the last sanity check before the file is
	// allowed to take the name nvgt loads.
	if (file_get_size(temporary) < 1024) {
		file_delete(temporary);
		announce("The file github sent is too small to be a library. The release may have no " + ASSET + " attached.");
		return;
	}

	// What was downloaded is checked against what the engine will expect, and
	// the check is on the first bytes because that is where the two platforms
	// disagree. A windows engine loads a PE file, which starts "MZ"; a linux
	// engine loads an ELF, which starts with 0x7f then "ELF". This is the check
	// that was missing when the wrong asset was picked: a linux library written
	// under the name nvmidi.dll is present, is complete, is the right sort of
	// size, and cannot possibly load - and the engine's only words for that are
	// "failed to load plugin", which names neither the file nor the reason. Two
	// releases went out that way. Refusing here costs one sentence and saves a
	// person an evening of swapping files.
	//
	// Read in two separate opens, and nothing is read while a stream is open
	// unless it is about to be closed in the same breath. The first version of
	// this check read two bytes and then closed a stream that had been read to
	// its end, and the engine answered with a bare
	//
	//     Yikes! System exception
	//
	// and nothing else - no reason, no line, and not the sentence this check
	// exists to say. That is the same class of failure as the plugin message
	// this whole block is about, produced by the check itself, which is worse
	// than not having it: the user loses even the information that arrived.
	//
	// So the byte count is asked for first, from a stream that is opened and
	// closed without a read, and the two bytes are fetched by a second stream
	// opened at offset zero. Neither call can disturb the other.
	bool windows_wanted = ASSET == "nvmidi.dll";
	int64 arrived = 0;
	{
		file counter(temporary, "rb");
		if (counter.active) {
			arrived = counter.size;
			counter.close();
		}
	}

	// Too short to hold a header of any kind; the size check above is in
	// kilobytes, this one is in bytes, and an empty file passes the first.
	if (arrived < 2) {
		file_delete(temporary);
		announce("The file github sent is empty or nearly so, so it cannot be a library. Please try again, and if it keeps happening the release is at fault, not you.");
		return;
	}

	string magic = "";
	mark("8 before magic");
	{
		file head(temporary, "rb");
		if (head.active) {
			magic = head.read(2);
			head.close();
		}
	}
	mark("9 magic read " + magic.length());

	// A stream that would not open is not a verdict about the file, so it is
	// reported as what it is rather than as a wrong platform.
	if (magic.length() < 2) {
		file_delete(temporary);
		announce("Cannot read the downloaded file after writing it. The path may be on a device that will not open it twice; nothing was installed.");
		return;
	}

	string looks_like = magic == "MZ" ? "a windows library" : "not a windows library";
	if (windows_wanted && magic != "MZ") {
		file_delete(temporary);
		announce("Github sent " + looks_like + " for " + ASSET + ". This is a bug in this script or in the release, not something you did - do not install it by hand.");
		return;
	}

	mark("10 copying into " + destination);
	// Only now is the old copy touched, and only once the new one is complete.
	if (file_exists(destination))
		file_delete(destination);
	file_copy(temporary, destination, true);
	file_delete(temporary);

	// The size is reported, not assumed. A copy that lands zero bytes produces
	// no error of its own, and nvgt's only words for the library it cannot
	// open are "failed to load plugin" - which names neither the file nor the
	// reason. Printing the number here means a person who then sees that
	// message can tell a truncated download from a library the engine refused.
	int64 installed = file_get_size(destination);
	announce("Installed " + ASSET + " " + target + ", " + installed + " bytes, into " + lib + ".");

	// The note is written last, after the library is known to be in place, and
	// it is the only record of which release this folder holds - see
	// STAMP_NAME. Announced too, so a person who keeps several copies of nvgt
	// can tell which one was just updated without opening any of them.
	remember(lib, ASSET, installed_tag);
	announce("Wrote " + STAMP_NAME + " in " + lib + ", recording " + installed_tag + ".");
}
